<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Transfer — Spike (recovery test)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Spike test: surge the transfer endpoint from 70% boundary up to 200%
boundary, hold 3 min, then drop back to 70% to observe recovery.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from sustained-capacity stress run, +2/30s):  28 VUs       ║
║                                                                      ║
║   BOUNDARY      =  28 VUs   (last fully clean step; 30 VUs returned  ║
║                              17% errors within 30s, 32 VUs was       ║
║                              100% errors — P2028 commit timeouts)    ║
║   70% BOUNDARY  =  20 VUs   (warm-up / recovery level)               ║
║   SPIKE (200%)  =  56 VUs   (the surge target)                       ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Sized at 200% (not 150%) on the same rationale as the other spike    ║
║ plans: 150% (42 VUs) would just replay the load test's just-over     ║
║ zone of overload, not test true surge. 200% (56 VUs) lands well      ║
║ beyond the measured breakdown — the stress run hit 100% errors at    ║
║ 32 VUs, so 56 is firmly in the saturated regime. The spike phase    ║
║ EXPECTS errors; what we measure is whether the system DEGRADES       ║
║ GRACEFULLY and RECOVERS once the spike ends.                         ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Load-generator memory: 56 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈ 56 MB     ║
║ peak. Trivial; default JMeter heap (1 GB) is fine.                   ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Spike ramp rate: +36 threads over 5s = ~7.2 threads/s — well         ║
║ within JMeter's comfortable scheduler range, no ramp jitter risk.    ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Virtual users are spread across MANY distinct (source, destination) pairs
(tests/stress/data/transfers.csv, 1000 rows) via CSVDataSet round-robin
(shareMode.all + recycle). The coprime-shift pairing (source[i] -&gt;
dest[(i + 499) % 1000]) guarantees every account is source of exactly
one row and destination of exactly one (different) row — contention
spreads across the full account graph rather than concentrating on a
hot pair.

This exercises the FULL WRITE path: FOR UPDATE on TWO accounts inside a
$transaction, TWO transaction rows written, fraud detector runs. During
the spike phase, Prisma transaction timeouts (P2028) are expected — the
test measures whether the server keeps the event loop responsive,
returns proper 5xx codes, and drains its queues once the surge ends.

Shape (Ultimate Thread Group, 13:30 total):

  ┌──────────────┬──────┬───────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ VUs  │ Hold  │ Maps to spec                    │
  ├──────────────┼──────┼───────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │   20 │  5 m  │ 71% boundary    (warm-up)       │
  │ 5–8 min      │   56 │  3 m  │ 200% boundary   (the SPIKE)     │
  │ 8–13:30 min  │   20 │  5 m  │ 71% boundary    (recovery)      │
  └──────────────┴──────┴───────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria:
  - During warm-up and recovery: p95 &lt; 5000ms, error rate &lt; 1%
  - During the spike: errors and elevated latency are acceptable —
    what we measure is whether the system DEGRADES GRACEFULLY (returns
    proper 5xx, drains its queues, keeps the event loop responsive)
    and RECOVERS to baseline once the spike ends. A spike that leaves
    the system degraded after VUs drop back to 20 is a failure even
    if the spike phase itself looked OK.

Recovery is the key metric for this endpoint specifically. The transfer
write path holds row locks for the duration of $transaction; under
surge, locks queue up and timeout. Once the surge ends, the question is
whether Postgres releases its lock queue cleanly and Prisma's
connection pool reclaims its slots — or whether the system enters a
degraded steady state where the recovery phase shows elevated p95 /
errors compared to the warm-up phase at the same 20 VUs.

Response Assertion: regex ^(2\d\d|422)$ — same as the load plan. 2xx
(completed or PENDING_WEEKEND queued) and 422 (business rule
rejection) are valid. 5xx, connect/read timeouts, and other transport
errors count as failures. assume_success=false to preserve transport
errors.

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms). Even with think time, the spike phase
represents a real surge: 2× as many CONCURRENT USERS hitting the
system simultaneously. Real-world spikes (payday transfers, fraud
event cascades, batch payroll) look like a sudden influx of normal-
paced users — not the same users firing 100× more requests. The timer
models the realistic version of that scenario, consistent with the
load plan and the stress plan.

⚠ Weekend caveat (banking-rules §4): transfers initiated on Saturday or
Sunday UTC are routed to PENDING_WEEKEND (status 201) and bypass the
full lock+update+insert path. If this test runs on Sat/Sun UTC, the
recovery characteristics measured here describe the queuing path, not
the real transfer path. Run on a weekday.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/spike/transfer.spike.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/transfer-spike.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/transfer-spike-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:spike:transfer

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts          (generates transfers.csv)
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(host,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(dataDir,tests/stress/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <ConfigTestElement guiclass="HttpDefaultsGui" testclass="ConfigTestElement" testname="HTTP Request Defaults">
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">5000</intProp>
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">15000</intProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${BASE_URL}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${PORT}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables">
          <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.implementation"></stringProp>
      </ConfigTestElement>
      <hashTree/>
      <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager">
        <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Authorization</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">Bearer ${sourceToken}</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </HeaderManager>
      <hashTree/>
      <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="transfers.csv">
        <stringProp name="filename">${DATA_DIR}/transfers.csv</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="variableNames">sourceAccountId,destinationAccountId,sourceUserId,sourceToken</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="quotedData">false</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
      </CSVDataSet>
      <hashTree/>
      <kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup guiclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroupGui" testclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup" testname="Ultimate Thread Group — 20 → 56 → 20 (13:30)">
        <collectionProp name="ultimatethreadgroupdata">
          <collectionProp name="row-baseline-20">
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-threads">20</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-delay">0</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-startup">10</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-hold">790</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r1-shutdown">10</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
          <collectionProp name="row-spike-56">
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-threads">36</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-delay">310</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-startup">5</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-hold">180</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="utg-r2-shutdown">5</stringProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </collectionProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="LoopController" guiclass="LoopControlPanel" testclass="LoopController" testname="Loop Controller">
          <intProp name="LoopController.loops">-1</intProp>
          <boolProp name="LoopController.continue_forever">false</boolProp>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
      </kg.apc.jmeter.threads.UltimateThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="POST /api/v1/transactions/transfer">
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/api/v1/transactions/transfer</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">false</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">{&quot;sourceAccountId&quot;:&quot;${sourceAccountId}&quot;,&quot;destinationAccountId&quot;:&quot;${destinationAccountId}&quot;,&quot;amount&quot;:&quot;0.10&quot;}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <GaussianRandomTimer guiclass="GaussianRandomTimerGui" testclass="GaussianRandomTimer" testname="Think Time — Gaussian (mean 3s, σ 1s)">
            <stringProp name="ConstantTimer.delay">3000</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="RandomTimer.range">1000.0</stringProp>
          </GaussianRandomTimer>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="Status is 2xx or 422" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="1729207179">^(2\d\d|422)$</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Server error or unexpected status — transfer flow broke under spike.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">1</intProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
        <ResultCollector guiclass="StatVisualizer" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Aggregate Report" enabled="true">
          <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
          <objProp>
            <name>saveConfig</name>
